@prompt
Hazel is a live functional environment. Every edit maintains program structure, and evaluation proceeds *around* holes (the hexagons), so there is always a result to look at, which may be a value or a partially evaluated program.

Check on this program's evaluation with a **probe**: put your cursor on the `+` and press **Cmd/Ctrl+E** (or right-click, *Add probe*). Two things appear: a green underline marking the probed expression (the **probe**), and a box at the end of the line holding its value (the **sample**). Hazel computed everything around the missing piece.

Probe commands are listed in the **Quick Reference** in the sidebar; new ones are highlighted.

# Task

These slides are loosely themed as parts of a simulation video game, Night Gardener.
A nightshade plant drinks 125 ml twice a day, plus a 50 ml weekly top-up. Fill the hole and watch the probe; you are done when it reads **1800**.

@hint
A week is 7 days.

@reference
### Holes
Hexagons are holes. Hazel evaluates around them. Fill one by typing a value.

### Integer operations
`(+ - * /)`

### Probes
- **Add / remove a probe**: Cmd/Ctrl+E, or right-click then *Add probe*

@hints
Fill the hole appropriately.

@code
2 * 125 * ¿ + 50

@test
test answer == 1800 end
